# Data pipeline environment

Annotated reference for the whole set: the repository-root `.env.example`.
This file lists only what `data-pipeline` reads.

```
APP_ENV=dev
LOG_LEVEL=info

# PIPELINE_DATA_DIR=data/raw/google_local
# PIPELINE_BATCH_SIZE=1000
# PIPELINE_WORKERS=4
# Minimum usable review text length. Shorter text is dropped from evidence
# while the review still contributes to the rating sample.
# PIPELINE_MIN_REVIEW_CHARS=20
# Target number of demo restaurants; clamped to [2000, 5000].
# PIPELINE_DEMO_TARGET=3000
# Ingestion bounding box "south,west,north,east". The shipped meta file is
# US-wide, so this is what keeps the corpus to the five boroughs.
# PIPELINE_BBOX=40.49,-74.26,40.93,-73.68
# Administrative boundary geometry used to label borough_guess. Fetch it with
# the command in ../data/boundaries/README.md; the checksum is pinned in code.
# Empty disables boundary labelling entirely.
# PIPELINE_BOUNDARY_FILE=data/boundaries/nyc-borough-boundaries-water.geojson
# Fail instead of falling back to the approximate borough bounding boxes when
# the boundary file is missing or does not match its pinned checksum.
# PIPELINE_REQUIRE_BOUNDARIES=false

# --- Shared with chat-service ------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL. Start it with: docker compose -f ../deploy/docker-compose.yml up -d
POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://platepilot:platepilot@localhost:55432/platepilot?sslmode=disable
POSTGRES_DATABASE=platepilot
# POSTGRES_TIMEOUT=30s
# POSTGRES_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=10s
# POSTGRES_MAX_POOL_SIZE=8
# POSTGRES_MIN_POOL_SIZE=0

# Local Ollama embedding
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=ollama
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=qwen3-embedding:0.6b
# EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=1024

# Texts per embedding request (1..2048; 0/unset = 32). --batch overrides it.
# EMBEDDING_MAX_BATCH=32

# One outbound request. Must cover the heaviest batch: representative-review
# documents average 2,878 characters, and 32 of them take ~52s on a local CPU
# model. Do not lower this back to 15s.
# REQUEST_TIMEOUT=180s

# --- Review digest stage (build-digests) -------------------------------------
# The offline restaurant-level review digest stage. It is off by default:
# unlike every other stage it calls a paid LLM API, so nothing runs until it
# is switched on explicitly.
# DIGEST_ENABLED=false
# Digest-dedicated OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Independent of the chat
# service's CHAT_* settings: different vendor, model, key, and rate budget.
# Required when DIGEST_ENABLED=true and DIGEST_PROMPT_VERSION is an llm
# version (digest:rules:v1 needs no model at all).
# DIGEST_CHAT_BASE_URL=
# Digest-dedicated API key. Never logged: Redacted() masks it and the config
# summary only reports whether one is set.
# DIGEST_CHAT_API_KEY=
# Digest model id. Pick a long-context (>=32k), Chinese-summarising model;
# tool-calling ability is not needed.
# DIGEST_CHAT_MODEL=
# One completion attempt. Generous by design: one call reads an 8k-20k token
# input bundle.
# DIGEST_TIMEOUT=120s
# Restaurants generated in parallel.
# DIGEST_CONCURRENCY=2
# Completed generation requests per second across workers. Keep this low so a
# full 3,000-restaurant run looks like background traffic.
# DIGEST_RATE_QPS=1
# Restaurants per page in the paginated run (also the --batch default).
# DIGEST_BATCH_SIZE=100
# Additional attempts for retryable provider errors (429/5xx/transport),
# matching the online client's default. 4xx configuration errors are never
# retried; they fail the restaurant and the run continues.
# DIGEST_MAX_RETRIES=2
# Generator version and half of the cache key. digest:llm:v1 is the
# model-backed generator; digest:rules:v1 is the zero-model eval baseline.
# Changing it regenerates instead of reusing stored digests.
# DIGEST_PROMPT_VERSION=digest:llm:v1
```
